# PCB Library Services | Qmax Systems

**Meta title:** PCB Library Services | Qmax
**Meta description:** PCB component library creation — schematic symbols, IPC-7351C footprints, 3D models, and BoM properties. Altium, Cadence, Mentor, KiCad.

---

## Hero

**Heading:** PCB Library Services

**Body:** A PCB design is only as reliable as its component library. Over three decades, Qmax Systems has built a verified, well-documented library of schematic symbols, PCB footprints, and 3D STEP models across 30,000+ components. Every part is validated against manufacturer datasheets and IPC-7351 land-pattern standards.

---

## Common Library Problems We Solve

- **Pin assignment errors** — Incorrect pin mapping is undetectable by DRC and only surfaces when the board fails.
- **Land pattern deviations** — Non-IPC footprints reduce solder joint strength and depress first-pass yield.
- **Height property errors** — Wrong 3D model height causes missed interference and invalid thermal calculations.
- **Missing BoM parameters** — Absent MPN, AVL, or lifecycle status forces manual procurement research and introduces sourcing errors.

---

## Component Properties Delivered

- Exact manufacturer part number (not a distributor stock code)
- Full standardised manufacturer name
- Resistance, capacitance, voltage, or functional description per datasheet
- Linked footprint name validated against the package code in the MPN
- Lifecycle status: Active, NRND, EOL, or Obsolete — from manufacturer lifecycle data
- Approved equivalent parts where alternatives exist

---

## 3D Model Formats

- **STEP (AP214)** — Universal neutral format, compatible with all major MCAD and EDA tools
- **IGES** — Legacy neutral format for older mechanical CAD environments
- **SolidWorks (.SLDPRT)** — Native SolidWorks part for ECAD/MCAD co-design workflow
- **Creo (.PRT)** — Native Creo Parametric part for PTC-based environments
- **Linked STEP** — STEP model linked to footprint for 3D DRC and clearance checking

---

## FAQ

**Q: What is included in a production-ready PCB library component?**
A: Three linked elements: a schematic symbol with complete pin definition and BoM properties, a PCB footprint with IPC-7351C-compliant land pattern geometry and full fabrication properties, and a 3D STEP model with exact manufacturer geometry.

**Q: What does IPC-7351C compliant mean for PCB footprints?**
A: IPC-7351C specifies land pad dimensions, courtyard clearances, and silkscreen conventions for over 30 package families using three density levels (Most, Nominal, Least). Non-IPC footprints produce insufficient or excessive paste deposits.

**Q: How does Qmax ensure zero defects in schematic symbols, PCB footprints, and 3D models?**
A: A seven-stage QC process: source verification, symbol construction, footprint geometry, symbol-footprint cross-check, 3D model verification, in-tool DRC, and release sign-off.

**Q: Who owns the PCB library components and associated data that Qmax creates?**
A: All deliverables — schematic symbols, PCB footprints, 3D models, property databases, and documentation — are the sole intellectual property of the customer.

**Q: Can Qmax build PCB library components for any EDA tool or CAD platform?**
A: Yes. Cadence OrCAD Capture, Cadence Allegro PCB, Altium Designer, Mentor PADS, Mentor Expedition, KiCad, and others.

**Q: What makes Qmax PCB footprints DFM-qualified?**
A: Stencil aperture optimisation for paste release, solder mask clearance verification against fabricator capability, thermal relief configuration for wave-solder joints.

**Q: Can Qmax migrate an existing PCB library from one EDA tool to another?**
A: Yes. Extraction of existing data, verification against original datasheets, correction of errors found, reconstruction in the target tool format.

**Q: What is the typical turnaround time for PCB library component creation?**
A: Individual components: 24–48 hours. Large-volume builds (50 to several thousand components): dedicated team assignment with phased delivery.